--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why AGPL-3.0 and not a permissive licence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This isn't a preference between licences. It follows from what Kolibre
contains, and any one of the three reasons below would be enough on its own.

  * Three files carry Calibre's algorithms and database schema (GPL-3.0,
    (C) Kovid Goyal), ported line by line, because a Kolibre library has to
    open in Calibre Desktop with no conversion step:

        backend/app/calibre/functions.py       title_sort, author_to_author_sort,
                                               sortconcat, concat, uuid4
        backend/app/calibre/schema.sql         the metadata.db schema
        backend/app/calibre/calibre_naming.py  how folders and files are named

    Each file cites in its own comments the exact place it came from.
    Calibre: https://calibre-ebook.com — https://github.com/kovidgoyal/calibre

  * The KOReader plugin (plugins/koreader/) is not a separate program talking
    to the server over HTTP: it imports thirty-five of KOReader's internal
    modules and extends its widgets, so it only exists inside it. That makes
    it a derivative work, and KOReader is AGPL-3.0:
    https://github.com/koreader/koreader

  * The `unidecode` dependency (transliterating folder names, the way Calibre
    does) is GPL.

The first and third reasons would call for the GPL-3.0; it is KOReader that
makes it the *Affero* GPL. GPL-3.0 section 13 expressly permits combining
GPLv3 code with AGPLv3 code, which is what lets a single licence cover all
three instead of licensing this project file by file.

The Affero clause is also the right fit, not merely the required one. The
plain GPL is triggered by distributing a program, and a web application is
never distributed — it runs on the server of whoever offers it. Section 13 of
the AGPL closes that gap: if you let people use a modified version of Kolibre
over a network, you have to offer them its source.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Other third-party material
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * BookOrbit (AGPL-3.0): https://github.com/bookorbit/bookorbit
    A separate self-hosted library project, by other authors. Parts of
    Kolibre's KOReader plugin are ports of its own plugin's code, trimmed to
    what Kolibre needs:
      - plugins/koreader/kolibre.koplugin/kolibre_catalog.lua
        and kolibre_catalog_widgets.lua — the cover-grid ("mosaic") view,
        from bookorbit_catalog.lua and bookorbit_catalog_widgets.lua
      - plugins/koreader/kolibre.koplugin/kolibre_menu_pin.lua
        — pinning the plugin's own entry near the top of the Tools menu,
        from bookorbit_menu_pin.lua
      - plugins/koreader/kolibre.koplugin/kolibre_capabilities.lua
        — the capability-negotiation shape, from bookorbit_capabilities.lua
        (reimplemented rather than copied, but the shape is theirs)
    BookOrbit is AGPL-3.0, the same licence Kolibre is under, so the terms
    carry over unchanged.
  * Interface components generated with shadcn/ui (MIT), which is designed to
    be copied into your own project: https://ui.shadcn.com
  * Lucide icons (ISC): https://lucide.dev
  * Geist font (SIL Open Font License 1.1): https://vercel.com/font
    The OFL requires its text to travel with the font, and the font files do
    ship inside the built site, so the licence ships with them: see
    frontend-react/public/licenses/Geist-OFL.txt, served at /licenses/.
  * The remaining dependencies (npm and Python) are under permissive licences
    — MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0 — and their licence texts ship with the
    packages themselves.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Content downloaded while the program is used
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Kolibre ships no third-party text or images, but it can fetch them on request
into your own installation:

  * Author biographies from Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0). The interface shows the
    source and the licence next to every biography.
  * Biographical data from Wikidata (CC0).
  * Author photographs from Wikimedia Commons: each image carries its own
    licence, stated on its page on Commons.
